<section class="sec sec--band" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Questions</p>
    <h2>The things people actually ask</h2>
    <div>
      <details><summary>Is this free? Why would I pay you?</summary><p>You can file it yourself at no cost, and RESEP clinics will help you for free. We are for families who would rather not spend three to six months chasing residency and employment records from the sixties and seventies. If you have the time and patience for that, do it yourself and keep the money.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much is the compensation?</summary><p>Up to $100,000, depending on the category. Manhattan Project waste claimants receive $50,000 or $25,000. It is one-time and it is not taxed. No one can tell you in advance what you will receive, or whether you will receive anything.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Which years does someone have to have lived there?</summary><p>For downwinders, at least one year of physical presence between January 21, 1951 and November 6, 1962, or the entire period of June 30 to July 31, 1962. New Mexico reaches back further, to September 24, 1944, because of the Trinity test. Someone who moved to a covered county after November 1962 does not qualify as a downwinder. Uranium workers are a separate category and these dates do not apply to them.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What is the deadline?</summary><p>Claims must be filed with the Department of Justice on or before December 31, 2027. That is the filing deadline, not the starting deadline, and the gathering is the part that takes months.</p></details>
      <details><summary>My father already passed. Is it too late?</summary><p>No. Surviving spouses, children, parents, and in some cases grandchildren can file. This is the single most common thing families get wrong, and it costs them the whole claim.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Which illnesses are covered?</summary><p>There are three separate lists. Downwinders have 19 covered cancers, uranium workers have 7 conditions of which 4 are not cancer, and Manhattan Project waste claims have 21. Kidney and bladder are not the same category. Melanoma never qualifies. Uranium workers are also covered for nonmalignant lung conditions like silicosis and pulmonary fibrosis. If anyone told you "any cancer counts," they were wrong or they were selling.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How long does it take?</summary><p>Federal processing generally runs six to twelve months from a complete filing. There is an expedited path for documented terminal diagnoses.</p></details>
      <details><summary>I was already denied.</summary><p>A denial is not always the end. Sometimes it is a documentation gap rather than an eligibility problem. We will look at what came back at no cost and tell you honestly whether there is anything there.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Are you the government?</summary><p>No. We are a private company. Not the DOJ, not any federal agency, and not a law firm. Nobody from the government is going to call you about this program. If someone says they are, hang up.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

